Warne,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on lodges, reserves and farms; we don't sell packages.

Buffalo Kloof carries black and white rhino, cheetah reintroduction and an anti-poaching team with cameras and helicopter patrols. That operation depends on field communications: camera and sensor backhaul, ranger contact after dark, and knowing a link has dropped before it matters. The connectivity that's described today stops at Spekboom's living area.

We've done a desktop terrain study of Buffalo Kloof from 30 m satellite elevation data and cloud-free imagery, over the proclaimed protected-environment area. It's attached, one page, no pricing. What it found:

- Two ridge high sites either side of the Kowie valley, at about 360 m in the north and 310 m in the south, 4.9 km apart with a clear path between them.
- A candidate carrier uplink of 11.2 km from the Makhanda high ground into the northern site, with a lot of clearance to spare.
- About 44% of the 73 km2 study area is in line of sight of those two sites. The valley floor is where the rest sits, so a third site or a relay may be needed once we know where Spekboom, Acacia and Khatu actually are.

None of the camps has a published position, so the study lists them as positions to confirm; a marked map from you would let us add them the same day. The site positions are desktop candidates until we walk them.

The next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look like, with the high sites confirmed on the ground. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours either way. If you share the camps' current connectivity invoices, I'll put Buffalo Kloof's actual payback in that report.

Can I call you for 10 minutes this week?

